es mental  health issues and reproductive issues  and someone who aims to eat intuitively  here are my diet rules and reminder this  is not a prescription on how to eat so  please do not follow my rules or  eliminate the foods that I reduce in my  diet these guidelines are the result of  years of individual data collection so  please speak to a dietitian to work  through your own body data all right  rule number one I don't drink juice I  mean unless there's a little bubbly in  there  but honestly I rarely buy or drink juice  juice was actually a big fear food for  me when I was struggling with food like  I condition myself not to drink my  calories no matter what and as a result  I really obsessed over juice and like  hyper sweet calorie dense drinks and I  would over consume them whenever I  allowed myself to cheat AKA binge but  when I started to give myself  unconditional permission to consume  juice I realized that it actually wasn't  really for me I find that it like coats  my mouth in a gross film that I don't  like it messes with my digestion a bit  and it usually just tastes too sweet for  me I also would just rather get my  fruits and vegetables with fiber now of  course there's always exceptions to this  rule uh sometimes I craved OJ when I was  pregnant so I'd have some then and  sometimes I crave it in the morning  after like a big heavy meal or if I  drink too much and in those cases I  drink juice but it's definitely not  something that I typically buy rule  number two I don't drink soda similar  story with soda and I've honestly tried  hard to test myself to see like is this  a lingering remnant of my past Ed but my  body just does not care for soda I also  don't drink diet soda so this is  definitely not like a calorie saving  thing uh but real soda again coats my  mouth in a gross film doesn't feel good  to my body or my sleep and diet soda  probably feels even worse I have nothing  against aspartame or artificial  sweeteners for the masses but I just  know that they do not feel good to my  gut and I also cannot stay on the  aftertaste I sometimes get this random  craving for like a really cold Coke or  Diet Coke but once I open the bottle or  the can and I get like one sip I'm like  nope not for me craving crushed I'm good  for another five months but when I was  struggling with my Ed I would drink two  to four cans of Diet Coke every single  day probably because I was trying to  suppress my appetite satisfy my desire  for sweetness and give myself some kind  of like energy from the caffeine so yeah  maybe this has some like PTSD elements  to it and that's why my preference is to  not have soda but either way it's  definitely enough for me dog number  three I don't consume Splenda or  aspartame now I've discussed the safety  on artificial sweeteners in the past and  I think they are fine and safe to  consume for most people but again I used  to be heavily reliant on them both from  the aspartame in the diet coke plus like  tons of Splenda that I put my coffee and  oatmeal every day when I actually gave  myself unconditional permission to eat  sugar and I kind of edged these  artificial sweeteners out of my diet my  taste buds actually started to change I  just didn't want things to be sickly  sweet anymore so even just a little bit  of aspartame or splenda tastes way too  aggressive for me I can't even have any  sweeteners in my coffee actually and I  also cannot tell you how much better I  felt without them so splenda for sure we  know is not sibo friendly and aspartame  it's debated but maybe because I was  consuming such large amounts of it via  Diet Coke just the sheer volume tipped  the scale and did not feel good also  about a year ago I realized that the  protein powder that I was regularly  consuming had Splenda and so I switched  to a Stevia or monk fruit based product  and I realized how much better my body  felt and the flexibility piece comes in  when like I'm offered something to try  or it's like a small portion I don't  obsess over it it's all good but I just  don't purposely make these sweeteners a  major part of my diet number four I  don't eat at chain fast food joints like  McDonald's  oh they toasted a good for me okay so  first of all this is definitely a  privilege thing more than a nutrition  thing like I literally have never had a  thought about McDonald's like oh that's  so unhealthy or oh that's so bad or such  junk I never think about the salt or the  fat or the sugar in these Foods I  honestly just don't really enjoy them I  live in a city that is full of really  delicious food options a lot of them  aren't necessarily even more expensive  or even more healthy than McDonald's  like I'll order fish and chips from a  greasy chip truck and basically drown it  in sugary ketchup like not a green thing  in sight before I go through a Wendy's  or McDonald's drive-through and honestly  I I know that meals like this like  devoid a fiber don't make me feel great  usually I feel a little sluggish after  and if it's dinner and I've got no fiber  especially if there's little booze  involved I will be constipated the next  day I know this and I'm going to talk  more about this in a minute so I  personally just do not feel like most  chain fast food is like worth [ __ ] up  my digestion for a day having said that  sometimes I crave like a McDonald's  McFlurry so I'll get that or if I repeat  in the boons without a lot of options I  would of course stop to have a meal but  it's just not something that I seek out  very often number five I pretty much  only drink on the weekend I mean if I'm  on holidays and like every day is the  weekend am I right but I do try to have  structure with my week specifically with  alcohol because I know that alcohol  doesn't add anything nourishing to my  body like food does even if I were to  compare that with high calorie less  healthy food so unlike with most foods  that I don't put limitations on I do try  to limit my consumption of alcohol to  weekends and never more than like two  times a week now here's the honest truth  I like an adult Bevy sometimes I like  too so I need to balance my own fun and  pleasure with the responsible part of my  brain that has to deal with the reality  of being a 35 year old mom and business  owner  I know that alcohol does not make my  body feel good it dehydrates me so  pretty much without fail I will be  constipated the next day even if I try  to up my water and it also really messes  up my sleep and my blood sugars in the  night so if I choose to drink I am  knowingly choosing a bad night's sleep  and like a frustrating morning just  trying to get myself to poop so out of  respect for my own aging body I try to  really only drink on the weekends when I  know I've got more flexibility in the  day that follows to just like make it  through so usually that's once Max twice  a week if I were to break my rule and  like drink on a Wednesday I wouldn't  punish myself like I used to when I had  an ed like this is not about saving  calories or trying to like burn off that  cocktail at the gym this is about  limiting the self-punishment of a  hangover in my Elder Millennial age rule  number six I eat protein and produce at  almost every meal and this is one of  those rules that I literally really do  not even think about I just intuitively  know that this is how I build a meal  protein keeps me satrated so that I can  get to that next meal without needing to  have a snack and I know that that's  important to me personally because  spacing out my meals has really helped  with my digestion and sibo prevention by  stimulating the migratory Motor complex  and then produce whether it's veggies or  fruit definitely helps me to feel  hydrated and gives me that fiber that I  need to stay regular once or twice a  week I will like eat out and have just  straight up carbs like pasta or pizza or  both maybe even dessert but again I  always know that this is probably going  to back me up the next day so it's not  something that I purposely do very often  rule number seven I don't skip meals  like never pretty much never like maybe  if I were to be very sick but even then  I would probably try to find something  easy on my tummy to get that fuel and  yes this may seem counter to intuitive  eating and it often feels like it is but  you have to remember that intuitive  eating is not just the hunger fullness  diet it's about self-care and I know  that there are a lot of reasons why I  might not feel very hungry one day  sometimes I'm sad sometimes I'm stressed  or tired or wired or I feel nauseous  because I have coveted or whatever or  something else I ate but I know that my  body still needs fuel and if I skip a  meal it usually catches up to me and  causes like a ripple effect of problems  after but if I were to skip dinner I'd  wake up in the night hungry which  disrupts my sleep and then I start to  have anxiety about not sleeping from  Hunger which exacerbates my insomnia for  days weeks to come meanwhile I get a  headache in the morning for under  fueling and my digestion the next day is  all thrown off because I had to eat  something in the middle of the night so  skipping one meal because I just didn't  feel like eating could potentially take  me days to recover from just due to some  of the other physical and mental health  issues that I have so again I know my  body I know that it needs consistent  fuel to operate at its best so out of an  active self-care knowing that I usually  actually feel better when I do eat  something I pretty much never skip a  meal and speaking of not skipping a meal  I also never skip my bedtime snack  honestly I just don't feel emotionally  satisfied without a snack before bed and  I also know that I likely won't make it  through the night without that last  post-dinner Nosh I was raised in a  bedtime snack on a family so this may be  partially habitual but growing up we  always had peanut butter on those like  simple little kind of basic crackers or  buttered French toast every night before  bed like it's actually one of my most  Vivid childhood memories like I can  smell and I can taste that toast right  now and today my kids have a bedtime  snack every night and I still cannot  like cannot go to bed without one myself  it's actually not that different from  the whole like I need something sweet  after a meal thing even if I feel  satiated I still want to have a bedtime  snack so this is basically what we call  mouth hunger so the need to eat for a  specific flavor or sensation rather than  the need to eat for like a physical  hunger and mouth hunger is a total  totally valid reason to eat judgment  free for me the Bedtime snack is a  combination of mouth hunger and  preventing physical stomach hunger in  the night to protect and preserve my  delicate sleep so yeah leave me a  comment below if you are also a bedtime  snacker rule number nine I move my body  every day pretty much every day even on  Christmas unless again I'm feeling sick  now that doesn't mean I do a formal  workout every day I don't obsessively  exercise every day but unless I'm really  sick and it will not feel good to me I  do move my body every day and this is  more for my mental health and my  physical health it's definitely not to  maintain or change my body weight but  regular movement also feels pretty much  Essential for my digestion and  regularity as well and if you want to  see what kind of movement I generally  get up to in my usual work week you can  check out my video right here but even  if I were to be stuck on like an  airplane or in a car I would try to get  up like stretch my legs stimulate like  my muscles a little bit because I just  know that when I don't everything just  slows down not good and Rule Number 10 I  keep unhealthy Foods in my house always  no exception doesn't matter where we are  a bag of chips or a carton of ice cream  is never too far away the years when I  didn't keep these kinds of foods in my  house were really the years when I  binged hard whenever I got the chance if  anyone's seen the classic binge scenes  in the show physical with Rose Burns you  know what I'm talking about now if  keeping bad foods out of your house  helps you in your Wellness Journey  personally then cool you do you but this  is an actual hard rule for me seeing  those foods every time I open the  freezer or the pantry has helped to  neutralize their power it's made them  just another food so it doesn't matter  if we're traveling or we're staying in  the cottage or Airbnb or here at home  I'm pretty much always fully stocked so  I hope that was helpful for you guys you  know some of you who maybe don't know me  very well were possibly expecting me to  say that like I never eat canola oil or  like a Subway sandwich or chemical Laden  Cool Whip but like that I'm a 90s kid  Cool Whip slaps am I right you can't  have a pie without Cool Whip Cool Whip  Cool Whip yeah these are the most honest  and closest things to rules that I have  in my food books and they're honestly  just so innate at this point it's just  kind of what I do because it's what  feels best to my body and my mind so I  would love to hear what some of your  personal food rules are that you employ  because it makes your body feel its best  and on that note that's all that I have  for you guys today if you like this  video be sure to give it the thumbs up  leave me a comment below with what you'd  like to see me talk about next subscribe  to the channel and I'll see you next  time on Abby's kitchen bye foreign  [Music]

